## Context
The Go source files in `src/go/` are not consistently named after the CLI
commands they implement.  `ticket.go` contains the core `Erg` struct, the
parser, and helpers — it is not a command.  `help.go` holds helptext data, not
a help command.  `author.go` holds author resolution, not a command.  This
makes navigation harder and violates the one-module-one-command principle the
user wants to enforce.

Current state (commands that do have a matching file: archive, check, close,
init, log, migrate, new, nextid, ready, selfupdate, validate).  Files without
a corresponding command: `ticket.go`, `help.go`, `author.go`,
`bootstrap_assets.go`.

## Actions
1. Audit every `.go` file in `src/go/` and list what it contains vs. what
   command (if any) it implements.
2. Rename files that hold shared model code to names that are honest about
   their role (e.g. `model.go`, `parse.go`) or consolidate into fewer files.
3. If `help.go` and `author.go` contain only one command's logic each, fold
   them into the appropriate command file or rename them.
4. Update `go build` / `Makefile` references if any file rename breaks them.
5. Verify `make test` passes after renames.

## Test
```
grep -rn 'func cmd' src/go/ | awk -F: '{print $1}' | sort -u
```
Each file listed should have exactly one `cmd*` entry and its basename should
match that command name.

## Exit criteria
- Every `src/go/*.go` file either (a) is named after the single CLI command it
  implements or (b) has a clearly descriptive non-command name (`main.go`,
  `model.go`, `testdata`, etc.) with no `cmd*` function inside.
- `make test` passes.
- A brief note in the PR body explains any file that was intentionally kept
  with a non-command name.
